 "result": "I created three files, but I couldn't build or run the image in this session, so none of it is tested.\n\n**Files created:**\n- `Dockerfile`: builds a Ruby 4.0.6 image and starts the API on port 3000.\n- `docker-entrypoint.sh`: the startup script the image runs before the server.\n- `.dockerignore`: keeps tests, git data, editor files, `.env` and `etc/` out of the image.\n\n**How the image works:**\n- **Build:** there are two stages. The first installs only the gems the app needs to run, leaving out the development and test ones. The final image adds the WireGuard tools and the networking commands that WireGuard's own scripts use.\n- **Run:** it starts in production mode with your existing `config/puma.rb`. WireGuard's files live in `/etc/wireguard`, which is set up as a volume so keys and clients survive a container restart. Port 3000 (TCP) is the API and port 51820 (UDP) is WireGuard. A health check calls `/healthz`.\n- **Workaround in the image:** the app reloads WireGuard with a shell command that only works in bash, but the base image's default shell is a different one, so that command would fail. The image makes bash the default shell instead of changing the app's code.\n- **Webhooks:** the server never sends webhook events by itself; something has to run `rake send_events` on a schedule. The startup script does this every 60 seconds, but only when `WEBHOOKS_URL` is set. You can change the gap with `WEBHOOKS_INTERVAL`, a setting I added.\n- **Warning:** if neither `AUTH_TOKEN` nor `AUTH_DIGEST_TOKEN` is set, the script prints a warning, because every `/api` request would be rejected.\n\n**To run it:** the container creates the WireGuard interface itself. It needs `--cap-add=NET_ADMIN`, the forwarding `--sysctl` settings, and a host kernel that supports WireGuard. There's a full example `docker run` command in the comments at the top of the `Dockerfile`.",
